**user**:
> Can I construct a regression models R2 from its log likelihood and its number of observations?
**assistant**:
> Not from the model log-likelihood and $n$ alone: $R^2$ requires residual variation **relative to total variation**.
> 
> For Gaussian OLS models with an intercept, if you also have the log-likelihood of the intercept-only (null) model, then:
> 
> \[
> R^2 = 1-\exp\left[-\frac{2}{n}\left(\ell_{\text{full}}-\ell_{\text{null}}\right)\right].
> \]
> 
> This follows because, under maximum-likelihood estimation of a constant error variance,
> 
> \[
> \ell = -\frac{n}{2}\left[\log(2\pi)+1+\log(\mathrm{SSE}/n)\right].
> \]
> 
> Thus the likelihood difference identifies $\mathrm{SSE}/\mathrm{SST}$.
> 
> Equivalently, if you know the full-model log likelihood $\ell$ and it is ordinary Gaussian ML likelihood, you can recover:
> 
> \[
> \mathrm{SSE}=n\exp\left(-\frac{2\ell}{n}-\log(2\pi)-1\right),
> \]
> 
> but you would still need $\mathrm{SST}$ (or the null-model likelihood) to calculate $R^2$.
> 
> This does not directly apply to REML likelihoods, non-Gaussian models, weighted regressions, or likelihoods with fixed rather than estimated error variance.
